Chlamydia
Young Adult & Adolecence
Mucopurulent discharge, Dysuria, Postcoital bleeding, Positive NAAT

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: YAA-006
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of severe pelvic pain and unremitting dysuria, requiring immediate assessment to exclude evolving Pelvic Inflammatory Disease or an acute surgical abdomen.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a rapidly escalating lower abdominal pain accompanied by severe burning dysuria and unexpected vaginal bleeding. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly necessary to rule out an acute surgical emergency, such as an ectopic pregnancy or appendicitis, and to initiate immediate empiric antimicrobial therapy to prevent ascending pelvic infection and irreversible tubal damage prior to the availability of routine daytime clinical services.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "It burns intensely when I urinate and I've started bleeding after intercourse, now my lower stomach is cramping constantly."
History of Present Illness: Symptoms commenced five days ago with a mild, intermittent dysuria characterized by a distinct burning sensation. Over the ensuing 48 hours, the patient noted a new mucopurulent vaginal discharge and an episode of postcoital vaginal bleeding. In the 12 hours preceding the visit, a progressive, deep lower abdominal and pelvic pain has manifested, radiating bilaterally and sharply increasing in severity, prompting the urgent request for care.
Functional Status: Ambulating with difficulty; bending forward to splint the lower abdomen.
Social/Safety Concerns: Patient is distressed regarding the sudden onset of symptoms and potential impact on reproductive health; requires immediate containment of infection.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 118/74 | HR: 96 | Temp: 37.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 64 Kg
General: Patient appears flushed, visibly uncomfortable, and demonstrates mild guarding of the lower abdomen while seated.
Physical Exam: Abdominal palpation reveals significant suprapubic and bilateral lower quadrant tenderness without rebound or rigidity. Bowel sounds are normal. Bimanual pelvic examination (chaperoned) demonstrates marked cervical motion tenderness and a visibly friable cervix that bleeds easily on contact. The clinical presentation firmly aligns with the diagnostic tetrad of mucopurulent discharge, dysuria, postcoital bleeding, and a high clinical suspicion warranting an immediate endocervical swab to secure a positive NAAT.
Environmental Observations: Patient has rapid access to a clean bathroom for specimen collection; living environment is safe and well-lit.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications. No recent antibiotic use.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Uncomplicated Chlamydia Trachomatis Cervicitis with early pelvic involvement - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the bilateral nature of the pelvic pain, the absence of migratory periumbilical pain, and a negative McBurney's point rebound tenderness.

Urinary Tract Infection (Cystitis) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the prominent presence of mucopurulent cervical discharge and postcoital bleeding, which are not features of a simple bladder infection.

Ectopic Pregnancy - Ruled out/unlikely due to a negative point-of-care urine beta-hCG test performed during the visit.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits acute, worsening pelvic pain paired with pathognomonic signs of an ascending sexually transmitted infection, specifically a friable cervix and mucopurulent discharge. The presence of lower abdominal pain and cervical motion tenderness signals early pelvic inflammatory involvement. Delaying clinical examination until the next in-hours period posed a direct and unacceptable risk of the infection progressing to severe Pelvic Inflammatory Disease (PID), potentially resulting in pelvic abscess formation or permanent tubal infertility. The urgent home visit permitted immediate diagnostic swabbing, exclusion of acute surgical pathologies, and the crucial first dose of targeted antimicrobial therapy.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Ceftriaxone 500mg IM stat and prescribed Doxycycline 100mg PO BID for 7 days (empiric coverage for Chlamydia and Gonorrhoea). Endocervical and high vaginal swabs collected and left for morning pathology courier.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent reinfection and facilitate tissue healing, implement a strict abstinence from all sexual contact for the next seven days, directly eliminating the mechanical friction that causes bleeding and breaking the transmission cycle. To ensure complete eradication of the bacteria, set a daily alarm to take the Doxycycline with a full glass of water, explicitly linking the routine to rapid pain resolution.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days to review NAAT swab results and conduct contact tracing.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department is required if the patient develops intractable vomiting, a fever exceeding 38.5 degrees Celsius, or severe, sudden-onset unilateral pelvic pain that causes fainting.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is empiric and presumptive pending formal laboratory NAAT swab results.
Clinical Course: The burning sensation and pelvic cramping should begin to noticeably recede within 48 to 72 hours of initiating antibiotic therapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if you develop a high fever, uncontrollable shivering, or if the pelvic pain becomes so severe that you cannot stand up straight.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The injected and oral antibiotics are designed to immediately halt the replication of the bacteria causing the inflammation in your cervix and preventing it from spreading into your fallopian tubes.
Actionable Steps: Complete the entire course of oral antibiotics exactly as scheduled. Do not engage in any sexual intercourse for the next week to allow your inflamed tissues to heal and prevent passing the infection.

